## Install the local guards (once per clone)

```powershell
.\scripts\install-git-hooks.ps1 -Owner <your-prefix>
```

This sets the clone-local `branchPolicy.owner` and `core.hooksPath=.githooks`.
It refuses to replace another hooks configuration. On macOS or Linux, run
`git config --local branchPolicy.owner <your-prefix>` and
`git config --local core.hooksPath .githooks` instead.

Before the first commit that adds the hooks, mark them executable so they also
run on macOS and Linux:

```powershell
git update-index --add --chmod=+x .githooks/check-branch .githooks/pre-commit .githooks/pre-push
```

Local hooks guard against accidents and can be bypassed. The server ruleset is
the real boundary.

## Activate server rules

`{{ ruleset_path }}` is configuration only. **Generating it does not protect
anything.** A repository administrator applies it once:

```powershell
gh api --method POST repos/<owner>/<repo>/rulesets --input {{ ruleset_path }}
gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/rulesets
```

After that, update the same ruleset by ID (`PUT .../rulesets/<id>`) instead of
creating duplicates, and read it back to confirm checks and bypass actors.
Keep the required check names and workflow job names in sync, or merging will
become impossible.
